# WS-WIDGET-OVERRIDE — Manual value overrides for Vega widgets

**Status:** draft v0.1 | **Last updated:** 2026-08-02

**What this is.** Two (eventually N) Workshop widgets render per-iSTEP numbers through Vega specs. Some of those numbers have no dynamic data connection, so authorized users paste values from a controlled sheet instead. This workstream is the generic override layer: **paste CSV → validate → persist in the ontology → widgets read overrides instead of source data when the toggle is on.**

**Decisions locked (2026-08-02, user-confirmed):**
- **Shared scope** — ONE override row per widget; every viewer sees the same overridden values.
- **Per-widget CSV contract** — each row carries its own `expectedColumns`; one generic parser validates any paste against the row's contract. Widget #3 later = seed one row, zero code.
- **Persisted toggle** — `overrideEnabled` lives on the row; editors flip it via action; consistent for all viewers, survives sessions.
- **`string(JSON)` values, NOT a struct column** — struct columns have fixed schemas and cannot serve widgets with different value shapes (same reasoning as the harness payloads). Raw pasted CSV is stored alongside for editor round-trip.
- **Validation failures throw.** Unlike the AI wrappers (catch-inside, because a failed generation must still leave a ledger row), a bad paste SHOULD fail the action: Workshop surfaces the message, nothing is written, the previous override survives untouched.
- **Saving auto-enables** (`overrideEnabled=true`) — pasting is intent to use. Turning it off is an explicit toggle action.
- **Contract-routed save (2026-08-02):** the controlling user keeps ONE workbook with two sheets (sheet 1 = Content Stability schema, sheet 2 = Quality schema). `SaveWidgetOverride` therefore takes **no `widgetKey`** — it matches the pasted headers against every registered contract and **updates every widget whose full contract is present** (a normal sheet paste matches exactly one; a combined table with all columns legitimately updates both). Headers matching no contract → error listing the registered contracts. Because an action is a single transaction, any validation error anywhere writes nothing to any widget. Editor gating is a **submission criterion on the action** (Current User ID in the editor list) — see the gating bullet below.
- **Editor gating via SUBMISSION CRITERIA (v0.2, user-proposed, doc-verified):** Current User conditions can compare "a user's ID … against static lists" — **individual-user gating needs NO group** ([submission-criteria](https://www.palantir.com/docs/foundry/action-types/submission-criteria), KB §02). So: a criterion `Current User ID is one of [static editor list]` on `SaveWidgetOverride` / `SetWidgetOverrideEnabled` / `ClearWidgetOverride` is the enforcement layer; when the Skywise group exists, swap it to a group-membership criterion — one Ontology Manager edit, no code. UI visibility uses a **static string-list module variable `overrideEditors`** (condition: contains current user). The former `editorUserIds` property, its `UpdateWidgetOverrideEditors` action, the reader's `canEdit`, and the in-function check are all **deleted** — the criterion is the single hard gate. Two consequences to accept: the editor list is maintained in two configs (the criterion + the module variable — keep them in sync when it changes), and criteria gate **per action, not per widget** — one shared editor list covers both widgets (fine per §6.1; if per-widget lists ever matter, that's the one case where the in-function check pattern would come back).
- **No harness integration.** No LLM anywhere in this loop → no `AIPInteraction`, no feedback, no evals. Plain CRUD.

**Transparency caveat (deliberate, per requirement):** non-editors never see the OVERRIDDEN tag, so ordinary viewers cannot tell the numbers are manual. If that ever becomes a problem, move the tag outside the gated section (one drag) and keep only the Edit button gated.

